"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 8.8 and positive surgical margins. The pathological findings indicate Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) with pT2c staging, but also note the presence of lymphovascular invasion. A significant limitation is that no lymph nodes were removed, meaning nodal status cannot be assessed, which widens uncertainty regarding risk stratification. Furthermore, there is a discrepancy in available data as the biopsy Gleason pattern and ISUP report are missing, while the prostatectomy specimen provided an ISUP grade group 2. The absence of postoperative PSA information prevents assessment of early biochemical recurrence trends. Future prognosis revision would depend critically on obtaining postoperative PSA levels and tracking their subsequent trajectory over time. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 37.67–87.91 months; its coverage has not been validated."
